 "observations": "In this run we observed an agent that, in every session from ck2 to ck6, first read the whole single-file industry.py (cat or Read: [2.2], [3.2], [4.2], [5.4], [6.3]) and then edited it with in-place python string-replacement scripts ([2.7], [3.4], [4.4], [5.12], [6.8]). Verification centred on reproducing spec examples: it saved expected outputs into /tmp and diffed them ([2.8], [5.6], [5.12]), plus ad-hoc python -c probes into SDE data ([1.6], [4.3], [5.17]). It extracted shared helpers twice (invention_sources at [4.4], invention_setup at [6.7]) yet also re-implemented the chance formula in invention_plan (ck4) and producer lookup in SDE.recipe_for (ck5). At [5.14] it changed the split rule away from the spec formula to fit an example, which later caused four split failures (ck5, ck6). At [6.11] it used cached bytecode of the old code as a regression baseline. It noticed the Jackdaw/Svipul relic gap at [2.9] and chose a Tech II layout, which failed hidden tests at ck2 and ck4. In its ck6 final message (after [6.11]) it reported a suspected error in the spec example rather than matching it. It never wrote unit tests or notes for later sessions."
